# TypeScript Clean Architecture for Enterprise Microservices

Modern enterprise microservices demand architectures that survive shifting infrastructure, changing databases, and evolving business rules. When building high-scale distributed systems using Node.js and TypeScript, tightly coupling your business logic to frameworks like Express or database clients like Supabase leads to brittle, unmaintainable codebases.

At [Abhishek Tiwari's Portfolio Blog](https://www.abhishektiwari.online/), we specialize in bridging the gap between high-performance systems engineering and robust architectural design. In this comprehensive guide, we examine how to implement **TypeScript Clean Architecture** for enterprise-grade microservices.

## The Core Principles of Clean Architecture in TypeScript

Clean Architecture, popularized by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob), enforces the **Dependency Rule**: source code dependencies must point only inward, toward higher-level policies. In a TypeScript microservice ecosystem, this means your core domain logic should remain entirely ignorant of external concerns like HTTP servers, PostgreSQL drivers, or third-party AI APIs.


+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        Frameworks                           | 
|         (Express, Supabase, Gemini AI, React 19)            | 
|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  | 
|  |                     Interface Adapters                |  | 
|  |            (Controllers, Gateways, Repositories)      |  | 
|  |  +-------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |  |                 Application Use Cases           |  |  | 
|  |  |              (Interactors, Services)            |  |  | 
|  |  |  +-------------------------------------------+  |  |  | 
|  |  |  |             Enterprise Business Entities  |  |  |  | 
|  |  |  |                (Domain Models)            |  |  |  | 
|  |  |  +-------------------------------------------+  |  |  | 
|  |  +-------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  | 
+-------------------------------------------------------------+


### Why TypeScript Excels Here
TypeScript's advanced type system—specifically structural typing, generics, and conditional types—provides the strict boundaries required to enforce architectural layers without runtime overhead.

---

## Layer 1: Enterprise Business Entities (The Domain)

The domain layer sits at the absolute center of your architecture. It contains pure TypeScript classes or interfaces representing enterprise-wide business rules and invariants.

typescript
// src/domain/entities/VectorDocument.ts

export interface DocumentMetadata {
  authorId: string;
  tags: string[];
  version: number;
}

export class VectorDocument {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    public readonly content: string,
    public readonly embedding: number[],
    public readonly metadata: DocumentMetadata
  ) {
    this.validateEmbedding(embedding);
  }

  private validateEmbedding(vector: number[]): void {
    if (vector.length !== 1536 && vector.length !== 768) {
      throw new Error('Invalid vector dimension size for enterprise pgvector storage.');
    }
  }

  public updateContent(newContent: string): VectorDocument {
    // Enforce domain business rules here
    if (!newContent.trim()) {
      throw new Error('Document content cannot be empty.');
    }
    return new VectorDocument(this.id, newContent, this.embedding, {
      ...this.metadata,
      version: this.metadata.version + 1,
    });
  }
}


---

## Layer 2: Application Use Cases (Interactors)

Use cases orchestrate the flow of data to and from entities, directing those entities to use their critical business logic to achieve application goals. They know nothing about HTTP, Express, or specific database drivers.

typescript
// src/use-cases/IngestDocumentUseCase.ts

import { VectorDocument } from '../domain/entities/VectorDocument';
import { IDocumentRepository } from '../domain/ports/IDocumentRepository';
import { IAIEmbeddingService } from '../domain/ports/IAIEmbeddingService';

interface IngestRequestDTO {
  id: string;
  content: string;
  authorId: string;
}

export class IngestDocumentUseCase {
  constructor(
    private readonly documentRepository: IDocumentRepository,
    private readonly aiService: IAIEmbeddingService
  ) {}

  public async execute(request: IngestRequestDTO): Promise<void> {
    // 1. Generate vector embeddings via AI integration
    const embedding = await aiService.generateEmbedding(request.content);

    // 2. Instantiate domain entity enforcing business rules
    const document = new VectorDocument(
      request.id,
      request.content,
      embedding,
      { authorId: request.authorId, tags: [], version: 1 }
    );

    // 3. Persist through output port
    await this.documentRepository.save(document);
  }
}


---

## Layer 3: Interface Adapters & Ports (Dependency Inversion)

To decouple our business logic from infrastructure, we use **Ports** (interfaces defined in the domain/application layers) and **Adapters** (concrete implementations interacting with external tools like Supabase pgvector or Gemini AI).

typescript
// src/domain/ports/IDocumentRepository.ts
import { VectorDocument } from '../entities/VectorDocument';

export interface IDocumentRepository {
  save(document: VectorDocument): Promise<void>;
  findByVectorSimilarity(embedding: number[], limit: size_t): Promise<VectorDocument[]>;
}


Now, implement this port using Supabase and PostgreSQL `pgvector`:

typescript
// src/adapters/database/SupabaseDocumentRepository.ts
import { IDocumentRepository } from '../../domain/ports/IDocumentRepository';
import { VectorDocument } from '../../domain/entities/VectorDocument';
import { SupabaseClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';

export class SupabaseDocumentRepository implements IDocumentRepository {
  constructor(private readonly supabase: SupabaseClient) {}

  public async save(document: VectorDocument): Promise<void> {
    const { error } = await this.supabase
      .from('vector_documents')
      .insert({
        id: document.id,
        content: document.content,
        embedding: JSON.stringify(document.embedding),
        metadata: document.metadata,
      });

    if (error) {
      throw new Error(`Database persistence failed: ${error.message}`);
    }
  }

  public async findByVectorSimilarity(embedding: number[], limit: number): Promise<VectorDocument[]> {
    const { data, error } = await this.supabase.rpc('match_documents', {
      query_embedding: embedding,
      match_threshold: 0.78,
      match_count: limit,
    });

    if (error) throw new Error(error.message);

    return data.map((row: any) => 
      new VectorDocument(row.id, row.content, row.embedding, row.metadata)
    );
  }
}


---

## Layer 4: Frameworks & Drivers (Express API Entrypoint)

The outermost layer handles incoming HTTP requests, routing, middleware, and dependency injection mapping.

typescript
// src/infrastructure/http/documentController.ts
import { Request, Response } from 'express';
import { IngestDocumentUseCase } from '../../use-cases/IngestDocumentUseCase';

export class DocumentController {
  constructor(private readonly ingestUseCase: IngestDocumentUseCase) {}

  public async ingest(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
    try {
      const { id, content, authorId } = req.body;

      await this.ingestUseCase.execute({ id, content, authorId });

      res.status(201).json({ status: 'success', message: 'Document ingested and vectorized.' });
    } catch (error: any) {
      res.status(400).json({ status: 'error', message: error.message });
    }
  }
}


---

## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)

### 1. Is Clean Architecture overkill for small microservices?
For simple CRUD microservices, Clean Architecture introduces boilerplate overhead. However, for enterprise microservices handling complex business logic, AI integrations (like Gemini AI), and specialized vector databases (like Supabase pgvector), it provides essential testability and modularity.

### 2. How do I handle Dependency Injection in TypeScript Node.js apps?
You can manage dependencies manually via composition roots or leverage robust DI containers like InversifyJS or NestJS's native module system to wire up your repositories and use cases cleanly.

### 3. How does this architecture integrate with React 19 frontends?
The microservice exposes standardized REST or GraphQL endpoints conforming to the interface adapters. React 19 consumes these endpoints via Server Actions or fetch wrappers, remaining entirely decoupled from backend database choices.

---

## Conclusion

Adopting **TypeScript Clean Architecture** in enterprise microservices elevates code maintainability, simplifies unit testing, and insulates your business logic from volatile third-party services. Explore more backend engineering insights on [Abhishek Tiwari's Portfolio Blog](https://www.abhishektiwari.online/).

