## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)

### 1. How do Redis Streams compare to Apache Kafka for Node.js microservices?
Redis Streams are significantly easier to deploy and manage if you already utilize Redis for caching. While Kafka excels at multi-terabyte log retention and complex stream-processing topologies, Redis Streams provide exceptional performance and low latency for standard microservice event distribution within a single Redis deployment or Redis Cluster.

### 2. What happens to messages in Redis Streams when memory fills up?
Unlike Pub/Sub, Redis Streams store data in memory (and disk if RDB/AOF persistence is enabled). To prevent out-of-memory errors, you should use maxlen limitation when producing events, e.g., `XADD stream-name MAXLEN ~ 10000 * field value`, which trims the stream automatically while keeping recent logs intact.

### 3. How do I achieve Exactly-Once Processing with Redis Streams?
Strictly speaking, distributed systems achieve **At-Least-Once** delivery combined with **Idempotent Consumers**. By utilizing Consumer Groups and `XACK`, you ensure messages are delivered at least once. Your Node.js consumer logic should handle idempotency (e.g., checking database unique constraints on `orderId`) to safely handle duplicate message deliveries.

